<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ./vx — the task runner. Deliberately standalone:

      * NOT in VortexArena.sln and NOT referencing any VortexArena project. It must build and run when the
        rest of the tree does not, because diagnosing that state is `vx doctor`'s job.
      * NO PackageReference. Every dependency it takes is one a fresh clone must already have, and the whole
        point of choosing C# was that .NET is the only unavoidable one. Adding a NuGet package here also
        means a restore must succeed before the tool that diagnoses restore failures can run.
      * net8.0 to match global.json (8.0.0, rollForward latestMajor), so it builds on the same SDK the rest
        of the tree pins rather than needing its own.

    See planning/bootstrap-and-task-runner-2026-08-01.md.
  -->

  <!--
    RollForward is the RUNTIME half of what global.json already does for the SDK, and leaving it out cost a
    contributor their first run (2026-08-03, Calculate Linux / Gentoo, dotnet-sdk-bin-10.0):

        You must install or update .NET to run this application.
        Framework: 'Microsoft.NETCore.App', version '8.0.0' (x64)
        The following frameworks were found:  10.0.3 at [/opt/dotnet-sdk-bin-10.0/shared/...]

    The BUILD succeeded — global.json's rollForward latestMajor let SDK 10 compile a net8.0 assembly. Only
    the LAUNCH failed, because the host's default policy rolls forward on patch WITHIN a major and stops:
    a net8.0 app will not start on a 10.x runtime unless it says so. Distros that ship exactly one .NET are
    the common case, so "install 8.0 as well" is the wrong ask when the tool is version-agnostic anyway —
    vx uses nothing newer than the BCL and has no PackageReference to constrain it.

    LatestMajor rather than Major: pick the newest runtime present, not the oldest that satisfies us, so a
    box with 8 and 10 behaves like a box with 10. Both shims also export DOTNET_ROLL_FORWARD as a belt —
    this property only reaches a dll that has been REBUILT since, and the shim is what rebuilds it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
    <AssemblyName>vx</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Vx</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <!-- The shim rebuilds this whenever a source file is newer than the dll, so a stale-looking build is
         never the user's problem to notice. Quiet output keeps `vx doctor` readable when it does rebuild. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
